```apex
trigger OpportunityTrigger on Opportunity (
    before insert,
    before update,
    before delete,
    after insert,
    after update,
    after delete,
    after undelete
) {
    fflib_SObjectDomain.triggerHandler(
        new Opportunities.Constructor(),
        Trigger.new,
        Trigger.old,
        Trigger.isInsert,
        Trigger.isUpdate,
        Trigger.isDelete
    );
}
```

**How it works:**

| Aspect | Detail |
|--------|--------|
| **Bulk-safety** | The `triggerHandler` iterates over every record in the DML, instantiating one `Opportunities` domain per row. Each validation method runs in O(1) per record with no SOQL. |
| **Record-scoped errors** | `error(message, record, 'Amount')` calls `record.addError(...)` on the single offending `Opportunity` and also records the entry in `fflib_SObjectDomain.Errors` (keyed by record Id + field) so unit tests can assert on it. Other records in the batch are unaffected. |
| **Rule 1 – negative amount** | Fires on both `before insert` and `before update`. A `null` Amount is allowed (the field is not mandatory); only a strictly negative value raises the error. |
| **Rule 2 – won opportunity amount lock** | Fires on `before update` only. It reads the *pre-update* stage from `Trigger.oldMap` (in-memory, no SOQL). If the old stage was `Closed Won` **and** the Amount has changed (`newAmount != oldAmount`), the error is raised. Transitioning *into* `Closed Won` while simultaneously setting a new amount is explicitly permitted because the old stage was not `Closed Won`. |

